  <h2 id="demand-now">Why arts demand is actually rising now</h2>

  <p>
    Arts students are still told the stream has no future.
    The data says something different.
  </p>

  <div class="grid-two">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>What the WEF Future of Jobs 2025 actually says</h3>
      <p>
        The World Economic Forum's Future of Jobs Report 2025 ranks critical thinking, creativity, and people management among the highest in-demand skills through 2030.
        These are core arts and humanities skills — not engineering-specific ones.
      </p>
      <p>
        The report also projects 78 million net new jobs globally by 2030, with strong growth in care roles, education, and human-support work — all fields where arts graduates have natural fit.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>India-specific demand signals for arts paths</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Psychology:</strong> India has fewer than 10,000 clinical psychologists for a population of 1.4 billion. The National Mental Health Survey estimates 150 million Indians need active mental health intervention. Demand for trained psychologists is structural, not cyclical.</li>
        <li><strong>Media and entertainment:</strong> India's media and entertainment sector is projected to grow at a 10% CAGR through 2026, with strong digital and OTT expansion creating content, production, and media analytics roles.</li>
        <li><strong>LinkedIn India 2024:</strong> Careers in humanities and social sciences are growing faster than many expect, especially in content strategy, education policy, and social entrepreneurship.</li>
        <li><strong>Law:</strong> Corporate lawyers at senior levels earn ₹15–20 LPA+. India's expanding regulatory environment (data privacy, ESG, fintech rules) is creating steady demand for legal professionals.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    The arts stream is not low-demand. It is low-directed. Students who pair arts knowledge with one marketable skill — writing, research, data, design, or people analytics — often find far more opportunity than those who wait for the degree to do the work on its own.
  </p>

